'''ansar, ansarian''' (an′sär, an-sā′ri-an), ''n.'' [< Ar. ''an-nāçir'', < ''al'', the, + ''nāçir'', auxiliary.] A helper; an auxiliary; specifically, one of those inhabitants of Medina who befriended Mohammed when he fled thither from Mecca, {{sc|a. d.}} 622.
{{smaller block|As for those who led the way, the first of the Mohadjers and the ''Ansars'', . . . God is well pleased with them; . . . He hath made ready for them gardens . . . to abide in for aye. {{right|''Rodwell'', tr. of the Korân, sura ix., verse 101.}}}}

'''ansate, ansated''' (an′sāt, -sā-ted), ''a.'' [< L. ''ansatus'', furnished with a handle (< ''ansa'', a handle), + ''-ed''<sup>2</sup>.] Having a handle or handles, or something in the form of a handle.

'''ansation''' (an-sā′shon), ''n.'' [< ''ansate'' + ''-ion''.] The art of making handles, or of fitting them to utensils. ''Jour. Brit. Archæol. Ass.'', XV. 69.

'''anse''' (ans), ''n.'' [= F. ''anse'', < L. ''ansa''.] An ansa (which see); specifically, in old ordnance, one of the curved handles of a cannon.

'''Anser''' (an′sėr), ''n.'' [L., a goose, orig. *''hanser'', = G. ''gans'' = AS. ''gōs'', E. ''goose'', q. v.] '''1.''' A genus of lamellirostral palmiped birds; the geese. {{smaller|The name is used with varying latitude, sometimes as conterminous with the modern subfamily ''Anserinæ'', but oftener of late restricted to the typical species resembling the domestic goose, such as the ''Anser cinereus'' or ''Anser''}}
